<DocsLayout title="Backup & Import">
  <h1>Backup & Import</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-slate-400">Export your configuration, restore it on another instance, or generate new pipelines from a JSON file.</p>

  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>Datanika supports exporting and importing your entire pipeline configuration as a single JSON file. This covers:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Connections</strong> &mdash; source and destination databases, APIs, files (32 types)</li>
    <li><strong>Uploads</strong> &mdash; dlt Extract + Load jobs</li>
    <li><strong>Pipelines</strong> &mdash; dbt orchestration (run, build, test, seed, snapshot)</li>
    <li><strong>Transformations</strong> &mdash; dbt SQL models with materialization, tests, and tags</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Export a Backup</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to <strong>Settings</strong> in the sidebar</li>
    <li>In the <strong>Backup & Import</strong> section, click <strong>Export Backup</strong></li>
    <li>A <code>backup.json</code> file is downloaded with all your connections, uploads, pipelines, and transformations</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Exporting requires the <strong>admin</strong> role, the same as importing.</p>
  <p><strong>The export never contains a credential.</strong> Every password, token, key and service-account file is replaced with <code>__REDACTED__</code> before the file is written &mdash; so a backup is safe to commit to git or attach to a support ticket, and it cannot be used to move a credential anywhere.</p>

  <h2>Import File</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to <strong>Settings</strong> &rarr; <strong>Backup & Import</strong></li>
    <li>Click <strong>Import File</strong> and upload a JSON file</li>
    <li>If any items already exist with the same name, you'll be asked to resolve each conflict:
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Skip</strong> &mdash; keep your existing configuration, don't import</li>
        <li><strong>Overwrite</strong> &mdash; replace the existing item with the imported version</li>
        <li><strong>Rename</strong> &mdash; import as a copy (appends "Copy" to the name)</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Enter credentials for any newly created connection. The result message names exactly which ones need them</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Import is non-destructive</strong> &mdash; it never deletes existing items. Only items in the JSON file are created or updated.</p>
  <p>That extends to credentials: choosing <strong>Overwrite</strong> updates the connection's settings and <em>keeps the credential it already has</em>, so re-importing a backup can never break a working connection. A connection the import <em>creates</em> has nothing to keep, so it starts with the credential field empty.</p>
  <p>If the file was exported from a different workspace, Datanika says so and asks you to confirm before importing. Moving configuration between workspaces is a supported thing to do &mdash; the prompt exists because doing it by accident is not.</p>

  <h2>Use Cases</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Migrate between environments</td><td>Export from staging, import into production, then enter production credentials</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Clone an organization's setup</td><td>Export, create new org, import, then enter that org's credentials</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Version-control your config</td><td>Export periodically, commit the JSON to git</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Generate pipelines with AI</td><td>Give the format spec to an AI assistant, import the result</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bulk-create resources</td><td>Write the JSON by hand or with a script, import all at once</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>AI-Generated Configurations</h2>
  <p>You can use any AI assistant (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, etc.) to generate Datanika configurations from a plain-English description:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Give the AI the <a href="https://github.com/datanika-io/datanika-core/blob/master/AI_IMPORT_GUIDE.md">Import Format Guide</a></li>
    <li>Describe what you want: <em>"I have a MySQL database with orders and customers. Load them into BigQuery, create staging models, and build a revenue summary."</em></li>
    <li>The AI generates a JSON file with connections, uploads, pipelines, and transformations</li>
    <li>Import the JSON via <strong>Settings &rarr; Import File</strong></li>
    <li>Enter your real passwords and run</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The AI never sees your real credentials. Tell it to write <code>"CHANGE_ME"</code> for every sensitive field; Datanika recognises that placeholder and does not store it, so the connection arrives with the credential field empty rather than holding a fake value that fails at connection time. (A file Datanika <em>exports</em> writes <code>__REDACTED__</code> instead &mdash; both are recognised, and the two spellings exist so you can tell an export from a file somebody wrote.)</p>

  <h2>JSON Format</h2>
  <p>The import file is a JSON object with these top-level fields:</p>
  <pre><code set:html={`{
  "version": 3,
  "connections": [...],
  "uploads": [...],
  "pipelines": [...],
  "transformations": [...]
}`} /></pre>
  <p>All sections are optional. Include only what you need.</p>
  <p>Versions 1 and 2 still import, so older backup files keep working. A file Datanika exports is version 3, which also carries an <code>org</code> object naming the workspace it came from &mdash; you don't need to write one in a file you author.</p>
  <p>Full field reference: <a href="https://github.com/datanika-io/datanika-core/blob/master/AI_IMPORT_GUIDE.md">AI_IMPORT_GUIDE.md on GitHub</a></p>

  <h2>Validation & Error Codes</h2>
  <p>Imports are validated before any changes are made. If errors are found, nothing is imported. See <a href="/docs/import-errors">Import Error Codes</a> for the complete list of validation rules and error descriptions.</p>
</DocsLayout>
